    "exampleHtml": "She <span class=\"vp-verb\">can</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">swim</span>. · You <span class=\"vp-verb\">must</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">wait</span>. · We <span class=\"vp-verb\">should</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">go</span>. · He <span class=\"vp-verb\">has to</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">work</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>can</strong> says someone is able to do something, or is allowed to. <em>I can swim. Can I sit here? Yes, you can.</em> The negative is <strong>can't</strong>: <em>I can't come today.</em>",
        "tag": "can / can't"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>must</strong> and <strong>have to</strong> say that something is necessary. <em>You must wear a seat belt. I have to get up at six.</em> Often both are right. <em>Have to</em> is more common for rules and plans from outside. <em>Must</em> is stronger.",
        "tag": "must / have to"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>mustn't</strong> = it is <em>not allowed</em>: <em>You mustn't park here.</em> <strong>don't have to</strong> = it is <em>not necessary</em>, but you can if you want: <em>You don't have to pay. It's free.</em> They are very different!",
        "tag": "mustn't or don't have to?"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>should</strong> gives advice: <em>You should see a doctor. You shouldn't eat so much sugar.</em> It is softer than <em>must</em>.",
        "tag": "should / shouldn't"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>She cans swim.</em> → ✓ <em>She can swim.</em> · ✗ <em>I must to go.</em> / <em>You should to go.</em> → ✓ <em>I must go. You should go.</em> · ✗ <em>Do you can swim?</em> → ✓ <em>Can you swim?</em> · ✗ <em>You don't have to park here.</em> (when parking is forbidden) → ✓ <em>You mustn't park here.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "After can, must and should, use the <strong>base verb</strong>: no <em>-s</em>, no <em>to</em>, no <em>-ing</em>. The form is the same for every person: <em>I can, she can, they can.</em> Only <em>have to</em> changes: <em>he has to</em>, and it uses <em>do</em> in questions and negatives: <em>Does she have to work? He doesn't have to work.</em> In the past, use <em>could</em> (can) and <em>had to</em> (must, have to): <em>I could swim at five. We had to wait.</em> More about the past in <a href=\"/past-simple\">Past simple</a>. For asking nicely, see <a href=\"/polite-requests\">Polite requests and offers</a>. A command like <em>Wait here.</em> is in <a href=\"/imperatives\">Imperatives</a>.",
